Complexity
For \(n\) nodes:
- Time: \(O(n)\) for serialization and \(O(n)\) for deserialization.
- Auxiliary queue space: \(O(w)\), where \(w\) is the maximum tree width.
- Serialized data: \(O(n)\) tokens, including null markers.
Your BFS approach is a good, straightforward solution. Its decoder, however, assumes the input string came from your serializer and therefore contains enough tokens for every non-null node. We can make that logic more robust.

Approach 2 — DFS Preorder with Null Markers
This is another standard solution and is often the easiest recursive codec to explain.
Idea
Instead of level order, use preorder traversal:
Root → Left → Right


For each node:
- If it is null, append #.
- Otherwise, append its value, then serialize its left and right subtrees.
We use # as the null marker instead of "null".

For example:
                 1
               /   \
              2     3
                   / \
                  4   5


Preorder serialization:
"1,2,#,#,3,4,#,#,5,#,#"


Why this is sufficient
Preorder tells us the next node to create. The null markers tell us exactly when a subtree ends.

During deserialization:
1. Read a token.
2. If it is #, return null.
3. Otherwise, create a node.
4. Recursively construct the left child.
5. Recursively construct the right child.

Code
import java.util.*;

public class Codec {

    // Serialize using preorder DFS.
    public String serialize(TreeNode root) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        encode(root, sb);

        // Remove the final comma.
        sb.setLength(sb.length() - 1);

        return sb.toString();
    }

    private void encode(TreeNode root, StringBuilder sb) {
        if (root == null) {
            sb.append("#,");
            return;
        }

        sb.append(root.val).append(",");

        encode(root.left, sb);
        encode(root.right, sb);
    }

    // Deserialize using preorder DFS.
    public TreeNode deserialize(String data) {
        String[] tokens = data.split(",");
        int[] index = {0};

        return decode(tokens, index);
    }

    private TreeNode decode(String[] tokens, int[] index) {
        String token = tokens[index[0]++];

        if (token.equals("#")) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(
            Integer.parseInt(token)
        );

        root.left = decode(tokens, index);
        root.right = decode(tokens, index);

        return root;
    }
}


This version uses "#" to represent null, including an empty tree, which serializes as "#" and deserializes to null.
