Complexity
- Time: \(O(n)\) for serialization and deserialization.
- Auxiliary space: \(O(h)\) recursion stack, excluding the string/tokens and reconstructed tree.
- Serialized data: \(O(n)\).
This approach is especially attractive because the recursive decoder is short and doesn't need a queue or parent bookkeeping.

Approach 3 — Compact BFS with Trailing Nulls Removed
Your BFS serializer preserves all null child positions. However, the trailing null markers often aren't necessary.

For example, a serialized string might end with:
...,null,null,null,null


After we've recorded all non-null nodes, the remaining trailing null markers can be omitted. Any missing trailing child tokens will be treated as null during deserialization.

This keeps the same BFS logic while sometimes producing a shorter string.
Code
import java.util.*;

public class Codec {

    public String serialize(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return "";
        }

        List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();
        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = q.poll();

            if (node == null) {
                tokens.add("null");
                continue;
            }

            tokens.add(String.valueOf(node.val));
            q.offer(node.left);
            q.offer(node.right);
        }

        // Trailing nulls are unnecessary.
        while (!tokens.isEmpty()
                && tokens.get(tokens.size() - 1).equals("null")) {
            tokens.remove(tokens.size() - 1);
        }

        return String.join(",", tokens);
    }

    public TreeNode deserialize(String data) {
        if (data == null || data.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }

        String[] tokens = data.split(",");

        TreeNode root =
            new TreeNode(Integer.parseInt(tokens[0]));

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        int index = 1;

        while (!q.isEmpty() && index < tokens.length) {
            TreeNode node = q.poll();

            // Left child
            if (index < tokens.length
                    && !tokens[index].equals("null")) {

                node.left = new TreeNode(
                    Integer.parseInt(tokens[index])
                );

                q.offer(node.left);
            }
            index++;

            // Right child
            if (index < tokens.length
                    && !tokens[index].equals("null")) {

                node.right = new TreeNode(
                    Integer.parseInt(tokens[index])
                );

                q.offer(node.right);
            }
            index++;
        }

        return root;
    }
}


